The math: the stack as formulas
With L_\sigma = G_\sigma * I the image smoothed at scale \sigma:

intensity L_\sigma; edges \|\nabla L_\sigma\| = \sqrt{L_x^2 + L_y^2}; blobs \sigma^2 \nabla^2 L_\sigma = \sigma^2(L_{xx} + L_{yy});
ridges: the eigenvalues \lambda_1 \ge \lambda_2 of the Hessian \sigma^2\begin{pmatrix}L_{xx} & L_{xy}\\ L_{xy} & L_{yy}\end{pmatrix};
texture: the eigenvalues \mu_1 \ge \mu_2 of the structure tensor G_{\sigma/2} * \big(\nabla L_\sigma \nabla L_\sigma^{\top}\big), times \sigma^2.

The factor \sigma^2 makes responses comparable across scales. Derived and explained on the edges, blobs and ridges day; here they are only inputs.

Recall: two Gaussians and a threshold
The thresholding day modelled the histogram as two Gaussians (object and background) and put the threshold where the two weighted densities cross: the minimum-error threshold (Kittler–Illingworth). It found the two Gaussians by trying every threshold t and fitting each side separately.
Today, the same model:

with K classes instead of two;
fitted by EM: soft assignments instead of a hard split at t;
giving every pixel a probability of each class, not only a label.

By the end of this exercise you should be able to:

fit and read a grey-level mixture: labels, posteriors (probability maps), Bayes boundaries, and their relation to the minimum-error threshold and multi-level Otsu;
explain why a histogram mixture gives speckle on noisy images;
explain why a full-covariance mixture keeps a shadowed object whole in colour space where k-means splits it;
read population fractions from mixture weights;
flag anomalies by low likelihood or Mahalanobis distance.

