Matching: one to one, at a threshold
Choose an IoU threshold \tau (often 0.5). A true object and a predicted object may be matched only if their IoU \ge \tau, and every object takes at most one partner.

matched pair: a true positive (TP);
predicted object without partner: a false positive (FP);
true object without partner: a false negative (FN).

There are no true-negative objects: the background is not made of objects. So no “accuracy” and no ROC curve for objects.

What k-means assumes
Every boundary is a straight bisector between two centres, and every point belongs to exactly one group. So k-means works best for groups that are

round (same spread in every direction): an elongated group is cut across, because cutting a long group in half lowers J more than separating two parallel groups;
of similar size: the boundary sits halfway between the centres, so a big, spread-out group loses its outer points to a small neighbour;
measured in comparable units: distances add squared differences of all features. Rescale one axis (µm instead of mm, or intensity × 10) and the groups change.

The next units replace the round, equal groups by Gaussians with their own shape, size and weight.

Live-wire: an edge-following cost
Intelligent scissors (Mortensen & Barrett, 1995; Falcão et al., 1998): the user clicks on a boundary, and the cheapest path follows the edges. The local cost of entering a pixel is low on edges:

gradient magnitude: low where the gradient is strong, 1 - G/G_{\max};
Laplacian zero crossing: 0 on the zero crossing of the Laplacian of Gaussian (the centre of the edge), 1 elsewhere;
gradient direction: a penalty for running across the edge direction instead of along it;

each with a weight, after Gaussian smoothing with scale \sigma (the edges day owns these filters). For a thin bright or dark line, the line response itself.

The Bland–Altman plot
For each image: the difference (auto − manual) against the mean of the two.

Manual 20, 35, 50, 65, 80, 100; auto 19, 33, 46, 60, 72, 88. r = 0.9995, yet:

bias = mean difference = −5.33 (a systematic undercount);
limits of agreement = bias ± 1.96 SD = −13.3 to +2.7: the range of the error for a new image;
the cloud slopes down: the undercount grows with the count (proportional bias, e.g. more merges in dense images). Then the limits mislead; compare ratios (or work on a log scale): auto/manual drops from 0.95 to 0.88.
